 How to Implement API Policies in SAP CPI
 - Use SAP API Management to define policies.
 - Apply policies at the API Proxy level (PreFlow or PostFlow).
 - Monitor API traffic using SAP CPI logs.

In SAP CPI (Cloud Platform Integration), API policies help manage security, access control,
and traffic management. Whitelisting is a key security measure that allows only specific IP addresses
or domains to access an API


 API Policies in SAP CPI (Including Whitelisting)
 1. Access Control Policy (Whitelisting)
 - This policy allows or denies access to an API based on IP addresses.
 - You can configure it in SAP API Management to restrict access to trusted sources.

 Example Configuration:
 <AccessControl xmlns="http://www.sap.com/apimgmt" async="true" continueOnError="false" enabled="true">
     <IPRules noRuleMatchAction="

<AccessControl xmlns="http://www.sap.com/apimgmt" async="true" continueOnError="false" enabled="true">
    <IPRules noRuleMatchAction="DENY">
        <MatchRule action="ALLOW">
            <SourceAddress mask="32">192.168.1.100</SourceAddress>
            <SourceAddress mask="32">203.0.113.50</SourceAddress>
        </MatchRule>
    </IPRules>
    <IgnoreTrueClientIPHeader>true</IgnoreTrueClientIPHeader>
</AccessControl>

- The above configuration allows only specific IPs to access the API.
- Any request from an unlisted IP will be denied.


2. Spike Arrest Policy (Traffic Control)
- Prevents API overload by limiting the number of requests per second.
- Helps protect against DDoS attacks.

3.OAuth & API Key Authentication
- Ensures that only authorized clients can access the API.
- Uses OAuth tokens or API keys for authentication.

